#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Tool-approval UAT — the E23 EXIT gate (plan §T7). One target, a Docker-free CORE + a Docker-bound JOURNEY
# tier + a CREDENTIAL-gated live tier, shaped exactly like E22's `code-and-ship`. The core is the
# load-bearing gate: it needs no Docker and no credential.
#
# WHY THIS TARGET CO-RUNS THE BACKING SUITES (the E17 T11 review's MUST-FIX 1, inherited verbatim through
# E19..E22): the bundle's per-case `status` is AUTHORED deterministic data. Only the entries carrying a
# proof block can be judged by a verifier; the rest carry shape fields alone. So `evidence-verify` on its
# own certifies "UAT declared green", NOT "UAT green". THIS SCRIPT is what makes the authored status honest:
# the SAME invocation that verifies the bundle also RUNS the suites that back the five cases it certifies. A
# red backing suite fails this target, and TestARedBackingSuiteFailsTheToolApprovalGate proves that
# mechanically via PALAI_APPROVAL_FAULT_SUITE.
#
#   Docker-free core (always):
#     1. go test ./tests/uat/tool-approval/... — four gates in one package:
#        a. the committed tool-approval-0.1.0 bundle is BYTE-identical to its generator and verifies clean
#           through the SHIPPED verifier;
#        b. the REFUSAL matrix: shape-consistent manifests claiming a property they did not earn, every one
#           REFUSED — including the MCP server's own `description` rendered onto the approval screen, a
#           denied call marked as executed, a run left waiting after its approval expired, and a merge tool
#           that grew a `pull_request_number`;
#        c. the promote gate: rc PASSES, stable is REFUSED, and a dropped claim marker still routes HERE;
#        d. the five cases' catalog, plus the HIL- orphan sweep and the guard that keeps `HIL-` inside the
#           only sweep in the tree that walks tests/uat/cases.
#     2. the approval SCREEN's own guards, the two widget surfaces, the modal route's ack budget, the merge
#        client and the approver principal — all Docker-free, all untagged.
#     3. evidence-verify RELEASE=tool-approval-0.1.0 through the shipped command (0/0/0/0).
#     4. the automation catalog corpus (the tenancy half runs in the journey tier — it NEEDS a real Postgres
#        and reports `ok` over five skips without one). E23 adds FOUR case.yaml files and edits three, so it
#        is not optional; the operator-doc corpus runs beside it because this epic's ceilings live in
#        docs/operations/approvals.md and known-gaps-1.0.md.
#
#   Journey tier (Docker, no credential — SKIP_JOURNEYS=1 to opt out):
#     5. against a throwaway Postgres: the E23 narrative where its seams already live — a gated tool call
#        that never reaches Execute without a human -> a run that PARKS instead of answering -> an approval
#        nobody presses that EXPIRES and releases the run -> an unauthorized principal who decides nothing
#        on EITHER surface -> an approved MCP write whose arguments reach the peer byte-for-byte -> a merge
#        that refuses a head which moved after the approval. Plus the TENANCY corpus, which needs this
#        tier's real Postgres to run at all (000044 opened a new tenant table).
#
#   Live tier (credentials from .env.local — `make uat-tool-approval-live`):
#     6. every leg E22's target runs, unchanged, PLUS this epic's: a real approval message posted into a
#        real Slack thread, and an opt-in real merge against a real GitHub App. Each SKIPS with the name of
#        the missing variable, so a PARTIAL handover reports partial-green rather than a red wall.
#
# HONEST CEILING (plan §6). This bundle does NOT claim that nothing dangerous can happen now. It claims:
# every side-effecting tool call CAN pass through a human's button; that human sees the CALL ITSELF and not
# a description — identity from the server, label from the operator, arguments verbatim, and the button
# bound to their hash; the run parks while waiting and does not wait forever; the approver is a project
# policy and not a Slack list; and no byte from outside — not a ticket body, not an MCP server's
# description — can write its own approval. NO TIER ADVANCES.
#
# AND THE PART AN OPERATOR MUST READ BEFORE TRUSTING THIS TARGET, which E23 T8 rewrote because half of it
# stopped being true. Through T7 the GENERIC half had no production decision surface at all —
# `slack.ToolApprovalMessage` and `coordinator.DecideToolApproval` had NO production caller, the shipped
# pump asked about PUBLICATIONS with E19's two-button message, and a gated MCP tool call parked its run and
# was released by the EXPIRY REAPER having asked nobody (filed as HIL-P8). T8 built it: the order-to-ask
# commits with the park, the pump chooses the screen from the approvals row's own discriminator, and a click
# routes to DecideToolApproval, which wakes the parked run.
#
# WHAT AN OPERATOR MUST STILL READ: nothing routes a Slack `view_submission`, so the deny-reason field in
# the argument modal reaches NO decision — a Slack deny is durable and releases the run, but it carries a
# constant sentence rather than the approver's own words (HIL-P10). And T5's ceiling is untouched: a write
# tool published WITHOUT `approval_required` silently skips the gate and nothing detects it (HIL-P5).
set -euo pipefail
root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$root"

echo "uat-tool-approval: [1/4] the E23 bundle + refusal matrix + promote gate + catalog + HIL- orphan sweep" >&2
go test -count=1 ./tests/uat/tool-approval/...

echo "uat-tool-approval: [2/4] the approval screen, the two widget surfaces, the modal route and the merge client" >&2
# -v because EXIT 0 IS NOT PROOF. HIL-002's authorship sweep, HIL-005's `sha`-on-the-wire assertion and the
# modal route's three-second budget all live here, and a reader has to be able to see them RUN — a package
# that compiled to zero matching tests exits 0 exactly like a package whose tests all passed.
go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestToolApprovalScreen|TestApproval|TestNeitherSurface|TestAnUnwrittenOperatorLabel|TestShowArguments|TestTheRejectedBlocks|TestTheActionableElementScan|TestNoFileButInteractions|TestGitHubMerge|TestMergeRefuses|TestAStalledModal|TestARefusedModal|TestAShowArgumentsClick|TestApprover|TestConfigPolicyInput' \
  ./apps/control-plane/internal/execution/ ./adapters/integrations/slack/ ./adapters/repositories/ \
  ./apps/control-plane/api/ ./packages/coordinator/ ./apps/control-plane/internal/identity/ \
  ./cmd/cli/internal/stack/

echo "uat-tool-approval: [3/4] evidence-verify RELEASE=tool-approval-0.1.0 (want 0 failed / 0 missing / 0 secret)" >&2
RELEASE=tool-approval-0.1.0 scripts/evidence/verify

echo "uat-tool-approval: [4/4] the automation catalog corpus + the operator-doc corpus" >&2
# E23 adds FOUR case.yaml files (HIL-001/002/003/005) and edits three (HIL-004, SLK-012, UI-002), so the
# catalog corpus is not optional — the re-verify rule this repository learned the hard way, twice.
go test -count=1 ./tests/uat/automation/... ./tests/docs/...
# The TENANCY corpus is the other half of the re-verify rule, and it is NOT run here: all of its tests
# t.Skip without PALAI_SECURITY_POSTGRES_URL, so a plain `go test` reports `ok` over skips — a green that
# certifies nothing. It runs in the JOURNEY tier below. E23's migration 000044 opened a NEW TENANT TABLE
# (slack_approval_deliveries), so for this epic it is doubly not optional.

# ---- the journey tier: real PostgreSQL, no credential -------------------------------------------------------
journeys="the E23 backing suites CO-RUN green (the gate, the park, the expiry, the approver, the MCP write, the merge)"
if [ "${SKIP_JOURNEYS:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  # Said plainly, because the closing line must never imply a tier that did not run.
  echo "uat-tool-approval: the Docker-bound tier is SKIPPED (SKIP_JOURNEYS=1) — the backing component suites" >&2
  echo "  did NOT run; this invocation does not back the bundle's authored PASS for them" >&2
  journeys="Docker-bound backing suites NOT RUN (SKIP_JOURNEYS=1)"
else
  if ! command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "uat-tool-approval: docker is not available; the E23 backing suites need a throwaway Postgres" >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  image="postgres@sha256:17e67d7b9890c99b055ba1e0d5c5be4ec27c9d3a72bda32db24a5e5d8a85af0c"
  label="io.palai.uat=tool-approval"
  container="palai-uat-approval-$$-${RANDOM}"
  # Throwaway local credential, generated per run; never written to a file, never echoed.
  password="palai-uat-approval-${RANDOM}${RANDOM}"
  cleanup() { docker rm -f "$container" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; }
  before="$(docker ps -aq --filter "label=$label" | sed '/^$/d' | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
  trap cleanup EXIT

  docker run --detach --pull=never --name "$container" --label "$label" \
    --env POSTGRES_DB=palai --env POSTGRES_PASSWORD="$password" \
    --publish 127.0.0.1::5432 "$image" >/dev/null
  ready=0
  for _ in $(seq 1 120); do
    if docker exec "$container" pg_isready --username postgres --dbname palai >/dev/null 2>&1; then ready=1; break; fi
    sleep 0.25
  done
  if test "$ready" -ne 1; then
    docker logs "$container" >&2 || true
    echo "uat-tool-approval: the journey postgres did not become ready" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  binding="$(docker port "$container" 5432/tcp)"; port="${binding##*:}"

  url="postgres://postgres:$password@127.0.0.1:$port/palai?sslmode=disable"
  # A DEAD Postgres for the fault knob: reachable-looking, connects to nothing.
  dead_url="postgres://postgres:$password@127.0.0.1:1/palai?sslmode=disable"

  echo "uat-tool-approval: [journey] the tenancy corpus against real PostgreSQL (000044 opened a tenant table)" >&2
  set +e
  PALAI_SECURITY_POSTGRES_URL="$url" go test -tags=security -count=1 -v ./tests/security/tenancy
  tenancy_status=$?
  set -e
  if test "$tenancy_status" -ne 0; then
    echo "uat-tool-approval: the tenancy corpus FAILED — a new tenant table and new case.yaml files must re-run it" >&2
    cleanup; trap - EXIT
    exit "$tenancy_status"
  fi

  # The BACKING suites. The -run selectors are the SHIPPED ones from scripts/test/component, verbatim: these
  # packages also hold suites this epic does not own, and running them here would make the gate's runtime
  # somebody else's problem.
  #
  # NAMING EACH TEST IS LOAD-BEARING and this epic paid for the comment AGAIN: E23 T7 measured that
  # `TestToolWithoutApprovalDeclaredIsBitUnchanged` matched NONE of the eight alternatives T1 added to the
  # allow-list (Go's -run is a regexp match on the NAME, and `TestToolApproval` is not a substring of
  # `TestToolWithoutApprovalDeclared...`), so the one test proving an ungated tool is bit-unchanged never
  # ran in the component tier at all. Every selector below was checked by RUNNING it and reading `=== RUN`,
  # not by reading the string.
  suites=(
    "./apps/control-plane/internal/execution|TestToolApproval|TestSlackToolApproval|TestExpiredToolApproval|TestToolWithoutApprovalDeclared|TestApprover|TestAGatedMCPWrite|TestApprovedMCPArguments|TestAJiraTicketBodyCannotApproveItself|TestAToolPinnedTwiceRefuses|TestMergePullRequest|TestPublicationFromSlack"
    "./apps/control-plane/internal/extensions|TestSlackApprovalModal"
    "./apps/control-plane/internal/store|TestSlackApprover"
  )
  status=0
  for entry in "${suites[@]}"; do
    suite="${entry%%|*}"
    selector="${entry#*|}"
    suite_url="$url"
    if [ "${PALAI_APPROVAL_FAULT_SUITE:-}" = "$suite" ]; then
      suite_url="$dead_url"
      echo "uat-tool-approval: FAULT INJECTED — $suite points at a DEAD Postgres; this run MUST fail" >&2
    fi
    echo "uat-tool-approval: [journey] backing suite $suite" >&2
    set +e
    PALAI_COMPONENT_POSTGRES_URL="$suite_url" \
      go test -tags=component -count=1 -v -run "$selector" "$suite"
    status=$?
    set -e
    if test "$status" -ne 0; then
      echo "uat-tool-approval: backing suite $suite FAILED — the bundle's authored PASS for its cases is not honest" >&2
      docker logs "$container" >&2 || true
      break
    fi
  done

  cleanup
  trap - EXIT
  after="$(docker ps -aq --filter "label=$label" | sed '/^$/d' | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
  if test "$after" -ne "$before"; then
    echo "uat-tool-approval: the journey tier leaked a container" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if test "$status" -ne 0; then exit "$status"; fi
fi

# ---- the live tier: credential-gated, NO CODE CHANGES --------------------------------------------------------
live="live legs NOT RUN (RUN_LIVE unset)"
if [ "${RUN_LIVE:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  if [ -f .env.local ]; then
    set -a
    # shellcheck disable=SC1091
    . ./.env.local
    set +a
  fi
  echo "uat-tool-approval: the credential-gated live legs — each SKIPS by name if its variable is absent" >&2
  # -v so a SKIP is VISIBLE: this family's signature failure is a green report over legs that never ran. The
  # merge leg is additionally opt-in behind PALAI_GIT_LIVE_MERGE, because it changes a real repository.
  go test -tags=live -count=1 -v ./tests/live/slack/... ./tests/live/repository/... \
    ./adapters/integrations/mcp/
  live="live legs attempted (read the SKIP lines above: a skip is not a pass)"
fi

echo "uat-tool-approval=PASS (E23 exit gate: bundle 0/0/0/0, the ungoverned-side-effect count re-derived from" >&2
echo "  the call ledger, the screen-authorship fence re-derived in BOTH directions, the park and expiry counts" >&2
echo "  re-derived from the run ledger, and the single mint recomputed from two packages' own source," >&2
echo "  $journeys, $live)" >&2
echo "uat-tool-approval: NO TIER ADVANCED — \`slack\` closes PREVIEW, \`knowledge-vector\` and \`apple-build\`" >&2
echo "  close DISABLED, \`console\` closes PREVIEW. Every counterparty is a FAKE. THIS BUNDLE DOES NOT CLAIM" >&2
echo "  THAT NOTHING DANGEROUS CAN HAPPEN NOW: it claims every side-effecting tool call CAN pass through a" >&2
echo "  human's button, that the human reads the CALL and not a description, that the run parks without" >&2
echo "  waiting forever, that the approver is a project policy, and that no byte from outside can write its" >&2
echo "  own approval, and that a gated NON-PUBLICATION call can actually be decided — the ask lands in the" >&2
echo "  thread and an authorized click releases the parked run (E23 T8; through T7 it could not, HIL-P8)." >&2
echo "  AND ONE CEILING AN OPERATOR MUST READ: nothing routes a Slack view_submission, so the modal's" >&2
echo "  deny-reason field reaches no decision — a deny is durable and releases the run, but it carries a" >&2
echo "  constant sentence rather than the approver's own words (HIL-P10)." >&2
